#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176729} :activity/id #uuid "eaad5957-8a77-5c3e-bc8d-81b1c2325346" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049190 :topic/id #uuid "e22ab26f-b369-5304-bfe9-74e3d0898875" :topic/math-academy-id 3633} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176672 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175946 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175795 :question/id #uuid "13cf1317-c3a1-5175-9f5b-c3da13cad348" :question/math-academy-id "e-6744"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dbed47b-77e4-53fd-8eb9-17ec13e42a3e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048978}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176569} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362791} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364427} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364434} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491127} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559872}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Generalized Eigenvectors of a Given Rank"} :step/id #uuid "78c5549c-e0d5-53d1-a816-9e9092586d36" :step/math-academy-id 24945} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176729 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176994 :tutorial/content "For some $n\\times n$ matrices, it's possible to construct a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ consisting entirely of linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix.\n\nFor example, the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nhas eigenvalues $\\lambda_1 = 5, \\lambda_2 = 4,$ and $\\lambda_3 = 2,$ with corresponding linearly independent eigenvectors \n\n![](images/45/451c66729d04278ec42f1ffcc558245b5cd2ef14b0c3d14dbd35447702b7b873.svg)\nThese eigenvectors form a basis of $\\mathbb R^3.$\n\nHowever, there exist $n\\times n$ matrices whose eigenvectors do *not* form a basis of $\\mathbb R^n.$ \n\nFor example, the matrix\n\n![](images/65/653166c6712102e8afacb5e2a73de617a3777e6812d9abbeb760f72b664525b3.svg)\n\nhas the single eigenvalue ${\\color{black}\\lambda=4}$ of algebraic multiplicity $3.$ However, there are only two linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, namely\n\n![](images/50/5007b5e49662ccb6aad508b233f4d107bdb89afc2607eee76280a16ec02d6f90.svg)\n\nSince there are only two linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we say that the **geometric multiplicity** of ${\\color{black}\\lambda=4}$ equals  $2.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "931f2f11-a4be-5b57-83cc-47ca54fc5443" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5420 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3961ed3f-bdbb-58d8-946c-67fb1150da8f" :step/math-academy-id 33796 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176836}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176730 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175984 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175835 :question/id #uuid "faa37d23-bdd2-5651-bf53-47835ee61cc8" :question/math-academy-id "e-9055"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfc9161a-5f14-5c16-afb0-832f81f30be4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050857}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176567} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362689} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362696} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491198}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Generalized Eigenvectors of Rank Greater Than One"} :step/id #uuid "fb412f74-1e1a-5c76-a91c-35d89b5cd5f4" :step/math-academy-id 33797 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176731}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176731 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176995 :tutorial/content "Recall that a generalized eigenvector of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda$ of  $A$ is a nonzero vector $\\mathbf{v}$ such that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{v}_{n-1} \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\ldots \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{v}_1 \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{v}_{n-1}, \\mathbf{v}_{n-2},\\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_{1} \\neq \\mathbf{0}.$ In other words, $A-\\lambda I$ maps $\\mathbf{v}$ to $\\mathbf{0}$ in $n$ steps. This is equivalent to the following:\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I)^n \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}\n\\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad\n(A - \\lambda I)^{n-1} \\mathbf{v} \\neq \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nBy the definition of the null space of a matrix, $(A - \\lambda I)^k \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ if and only if $\\mathbf{v} \\in \\text{Null}(A-\\lambda I)^k.$\nThis, in turn, means that a generalized eigenvector of rank $n$ lies in $\\text{Null}(A-\\lambda I)^n$ but not in $\\text{Null}(A-\\lambda I)^{n-1}.$ \n\n![](images/8b/8b0e00c74440c269a1d1b1c076aad56dfe3a056a1493c28862e4d080b5c33563.png)\n\nFor example, suppose that a matrix $A$ has the characteristic polynomial \n\n$$\np(\\lambda) = (5-\\lambda)^6(\\lambda-3)^2(\\lambda+4)^5.\n$$\n\nIn addition, let's assume the following:\n\n- The nullity of $(A+4I)^3$ equals $2.$\n\n- The nullity of $(A+4I)^4$ equals $3.$\n\nIf a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^{13}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = -4?$\n\nFor the given matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda = -4,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ is a vector $\\mathbf{v}$ such that \n\n$$\n(A + 4I)^4 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}\n\\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad\n(A + 4I)^3 \\mathbf{v} \\neq \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(A+4I)^3$ has dimension $2.$ This means that the solutions to $(A +4I)^3 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ form a subspace of dimension $2.$\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(A+4I)^4$ has dimension $3.$ This means that the solutions to $(A +4I)^4 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ form a subspace of dimension $3.$\n\nNow, since $\\text{Null}(A+4I)^3$ is a subspace of dimension $2$ in $\\text{Null}(A+4I)^4$, which is a subspace of dimension $3,$ our basis can contain only $1$ vector that lies in $\\text{Null}(A+4I)^4$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(A+4I)^3.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A+4I)^4 - \\text{nullity}(A+4I)^3 = 3-2=1\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-4.$\n\nIn general, we have the following theorem:\n\n*The number of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda$ of a matrix $A$ is equal to \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-\\lambda I)^n - \\text{nullity}(A-\\lambda I)^{n-1}.\n$$\n\n*" :tutorial/id #uuid "e3504196-e96f-544f-89f9-a985b82f8ddb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5421 :tutorial/title "The Number of Generalized Eigenvectors of a Given Rank for a Given Eigenvalue"} :step/id #uuid "cbffc464-3ec8-51a7-bad6-e40d2c96d1fe" :step/math-academy-id 33798 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176743}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176743 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175993 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175844 :question/id #uuid "bed5f45f-5c76-542b-965f-eadf474823c7" :question/math-academy-id "e-9125"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85ecbb00-cabe-5838-9ac4-50ebc8821c05" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050996}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176232} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176251} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362703} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491152} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501145}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Generalized Eigenvectors of a Given Rank Using Nullity"} :step/id #uuid "32f06230-5f13-50e6-adb6-a7143bdba331" :step/math-academy-id 34012 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176672}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176835 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177052 :tutorial/content "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has the characteristic polynomial \n\n$$\np(\\lambda) = \\lambda^4 (1-\\lambda)^3(1+\\lambda)^7,\n$$\n\nand that there are precisely $4$ linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda = -1.$ \n\nIf a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^{14}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = -1?$\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- In the characteristic polynomial, the root $\\lambda =-1$ has multiplicity $7.$ So, the *algebraic* multiplicity of $\\lambda = -1$ is $7.$\n\n- However, we're told that there are only $4$ eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda = -1.$ So, the *geometric* multiplicity of $\\lambda = -1$ is $4.$\n\nTherefore, the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ corresponding to $\\lambda = -1$ is \n\n$$\n7-4=3.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ccaaceee-ceed-5a3f-be84-18703a3e27a4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6126 :tutorial/title "A Second Example"} :step/id #uuid "b8ab2409-9530-5042-8db2-6e6b344401ac" :step/math-academy-id 37212 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176730}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176836 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177053 :tutorial/content "So, not every $n\\times n$ matrix has the property that its eigenvectors form a basis of $\\mathbb R^n.$\n\nDespite this, we have the following theorem:\n\n*For any real $n \\times n$ matrix, there is a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ consisting of generalized eigenvectors of the matrix.*\n\nTo illustrate, let's go back to our matrix \n![](images/d0/d09805a20bbc23954fdec5a893e05ec0bad45a6503e27354df60b4d71952f990.svg)\n\nwhere the eigenvectors corresponding to the single eigenvalue $\\lambda = 4$ are\n\n![](images/e6/e6053d03e5e0f4bc843856d88dbc9e8e14252200a5d7280a4a05872b19bd4d56.svg)\n\nRemember that $\\lambda = 4$ has *algebraic* multiplicity $3$ and *geometric* multiplicity $2.$\n\nNow, it's easy to show that $A$ has a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ given by\n\n![](images/89/890d3b69aa88f0f442760d03afc7333d2e918b5b3401a33b167cfa40321a5f4c.svg)\nand our generalized eigenvectors form the following chains:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nClearly, ${\\color{blue}{\\mathbf{u}}}, {\\color{blue}{\\mathbf{v}}},$ and $\\color{red}\\mathbf{w}$ form a basis of $\\mathbb R^3.$ In particular, our basis consists of\n\n- two eigenvectors ($\\color{blue}\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}$), and\n\n- one generalized eigenvector whose rank is greater than one ($\\color{red}\\mathbf{w}$).\n\nNotice that the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{algebraic multiplicity}-\\text{geometric multiplicity} & =3-2=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is no coincidence. In general, we have the following theorem:\n\n*The number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ that correspond to an eigenvalue $\\lambda$ equals the difference between the algebraic multiplicity and the geometric multiplicity of $\\lambda.$*" :tutorial/id #uuid "ad92ba79-e1b7-5818-9d69-6fd643580cdb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6127 :tutorial/title "Forming a Basis Using Generalized Eigenvectors"} :step/id #uuid "5899fe18-80e2-5145-909a-f3f95dbb69db" :step/math-academy-id 37213 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176835}}] :activity/title "Ranks of Generalized Eigenvectors" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176150}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501245} :activity/id #uuid "c193cfcf-aa19-53c0-9e94-a4fca834c89a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049190 :topic/id #uuid "e22ab26f-b369-5304-bfe9-74e3d0898875" :topic/math-academy-id 3633} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501245 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364434 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7df20e00-8b95-5d9c-9ad7-b28badfc60f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364436} {:answer/id #uuid "95cab23e-3490-5b26-810e-f8f4b6bb69ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364437} {:answer/id #uuid "9ebef18c-06dd-5776-88b8-f04701e33f5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364438} {:answer/id #uuid "25f77071-51f5-53cc-b0f7-f53de32e9e3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364439} {:answer/id #uuid "7ee16813-be34-5924-a753-6535fdc0bd29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364440}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364436} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d5de73d-f30c-59bc-9085-0f3566bc1afd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364435}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d180b2be-eb75-50b7-9052-2821851f23f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-78494" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -5)^{19},$ the rank of $(A-5I)^{5}$ equals $12,$ and the rank of $(A-5I)^{6}$ equals $9.$ If a basis of ${R}^{20}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $6$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5?$" :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =5,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $6$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(A-5I)^{6}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-5I)^{5}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ must be a $20\\times 20$ matrix.\n\n- Since the rank of $(A-5I)^{5}$ equals $12,$ then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-5I)^{5}=20-12=8.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(A-5I)^{5}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(A-5I)^{5}$ of dimension $8$ in ${R}^{20}.$\n\n- Since the rank of $(A-5I)^{6}$ equals $9,$  then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-5I)^{6}=20-9=11.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(A-5I)^{6}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(A-5I)^{6}$ of dimension $11$ in ${R}^{20}.$\n\nSince $\\text{Null}(A-2I)^{5}$ is a subspace of dimension $8$ in $\\text{Null}(A-5I)^{6}$, which is a subspace of dimension $11,$ our basis can contain only $3$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(A-5I)^{6}$ but do not lie in $\\text{Null}(A-5I)^{5}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-5I)^{6}-\\text{nullity}(A-5I)^{5}=11-8=3\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvectors of rank $6$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5.$"} :step/id #uuid "08414635-bff3-5a7f-b66e-3fbe87007867" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501278}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501278 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176232 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9915c4f7-fec8-5821-a1e5-91cb9f24bded" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375715} {:answer/id #uuid "fbb84d03-63e5-5ac4-bfb3-a8e737d4a931" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375716} {:answer/id #uuid "0d440d47-6467-5c2e-935a-ea22542facfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375717} {:answer/id #uuid "c1183feb-ea61-579f-895e-c9fbfacb3ccf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375718} {:answer/id #uuid "d954d438-acb2-5e8f-94fb-1b1426a9576e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375719}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375717} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce26ad16-b594-507c-bab8-79b00e68ef59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375714}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b2323904-dbe3-5d6e-b9ad-cd7ee2cf8fc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-109121" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=\\lambda (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -5)^{7},$ the nullity of $(A-5I)^{2}$ equals $1,$ and the nullity of $(A-5I)^{3}$ equals $2.$ If a basis of ${R}^{9}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5?$" :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =5,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(A-5I)^{3}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-5I)^{2}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(A-5I)^{2}$ has dimension $1.$ This means that the solutions to $(A-5I)^{2}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $1.$\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(A-5I)^{3}$ has dimension $2.$ This means that the solutions to $(A-5I)^{3}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $2.$\n\nNow, since $\\text{Null}(A-5I)^{2}$ is a subspace of dimension $1$ in $\\text{Null}(A-5I)^{3}$, which is a subspace of dimension $2,$ our basis can contain only $1$ vector that lies in $\\text{Null}(A-5I)^{3}$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(A-5I)^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-5I)^{3}-\\text{nullity}(A-5I)^{2}=2-1=1\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5.$"} :step/id #uuid "8dc09aaa-a187-577a-a919-a2d9e3f5ef8f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501279}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501279 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501145 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1997f878-2762-56d9-ac49-b26b9ac9edc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500521} {:answer/id #uuid "379bda0c-41bc-5297-b017-d518173b466b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500580} {:answer/id #uuid "58f930ba-330b-56ca-943a-67237cb474f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500648} {:answer/id #uuid "84bfa36c-7054-5b53-81f3-b9e1906d6186" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500742} {:answer/id #uuid "c9c4aa88-43ec-5d07-ae83-df734fe4814c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500894}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500648} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc7e5239-25ea-5909-b9ce-6ad812ed18c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501131}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "18821d40-33b7-5a84-97bc-878b35f96c25" :question/math-academy-id "q-109747" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $B$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{4}(\\lambda -\\pi )^{5}(\\lambda +3)^{2},$ the nullity of $(B-\\pi I)^{2}$ equals $2,$ and the nullity of $(B-\\pi I)^{3}$ equals $3.$ If a basis of ${R}^{11}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $B$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\pi ?$" :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $B$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\pi ,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(B-\\pi I)^{3}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(B-\\pi I)^{2}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(B-\\pi I)^{2}$ has dimension $2.$ This means that the solutions to $(B-\\pi I)^{2}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $2.$\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(B-\\pi I)^{3}$ has dimension $3.$ This means that the solutions to $(B-\\pi I)^{3}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $3.$\n\nNow, since $\\text{Null}(B-\\pi I)^{2}$ is a subspace of dimension $2$ in $\\text{Null}(B-\\pi I)^{3}$, which is a subspace of dimension $3,$ our basis can contain only $1$ vector that lies in $\\text{Null}(B-\\pi I)^{3}$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(B-\\pi I)^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(B-\\pi I)^{3}-\\text{nullity}(B-\\pi I)^{2}=3-2=1\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\pi .$"} :step/id #uuid "96d9823d-b5f4-5ae5-9b3d-4022dff3b3dd"}] :activity/title "Ranks of Generalized Eigenvectors" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500467}]}
